course: dictionary
lesson: 10945
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can mean different things in different places. The words around it, its context, tell you which meaning is right. «From the 1820s steam trains pulled long lines of wagons on iron rails.» Rails are tracks for trains, so here a wagon is a truck on a railway.
explain: Look for clues in the sentence. «In 1907 the Caravan Club was started, so caravan holidays were a new fashion» is about fun trips, not railways. Always ask: which meaning fits here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Some meanings are only used in certain countries or phrases. «Never climb on trains, wagons or railway bridges» uses wagon for a railway truck, which is common in the UK. In the US, a child's little red wagon is a toy cart you pull by a handle. «On the wagon» is an informal phrase that means not drinking alcohol.
fact: A wagon is a vehicle with four wheels, pulled by horses or oxen, used to carry heavy loads or people.
fact: In Britain, a wagon can also be an open railway truck that carries goods.
fact: «Wagon» can also mean a station wagon, a car with a long back, in informal US English.
fact: The UK spelling «waggon» is an older form of the word.
fact: «On the wagon» is an informal phrase meaning not drinking alcohol.
exercise: «Romany families had lived and travelled in painted horse-drawn wagons for many years.» What is a horse-drawn wagon? | A toy cart pulled by a handle | A small boat on a river | *A vehicle with wheels, pulled by horses, to carry people or loads | A railway truck full of coal
exercise: «A freight train is a long line of wagons pulled by a strong locomotive, carrying coal, containers, cars or stone.» What do the wagons carry? | Only food for the driver | *Coal, containers, cars or stone | Water to boil | People on holiday
exercise: «Never go onto railway tracks or climb on trains, wagons or railway bridges.» Which meaning of wagon fits here? | A car with a long back | A wagon in a film about cowboys | *A railway truck | A child's small cart pulled by a handle
test: Jake says, «My uncle has a station wagon for the family trips.» What does station wagon mean? | A railway station | *A car with a long back | A toy cart pulled by a handle | A horse-drawn cart
test: Mum says, «Dad is on the wagon this month.» What does on the wagon mean? | Pulling a heavy load | *Not drinking alcohol | Riding in a wagon to the shops | Working on the railways
exam: «In the 1800s the USA spread west to the Pacific with wagon trains, gold rushes and railways.» What is a wagon train? | *A long line of wagons travelling together, pulled by animals | A shop that sells wagons | A line of trains on iron rails | A group of children playing with toy carts
